# Retained opaque-field constructor bound: staged proposal

This component analyzes the exact `OpaqueField::new` bodies in the retained
portable and accelerated ARM64 standalone workers. It replaces one opaque
constructor term with a derived local ordinary-path contribution and named lower
costs. It is not a complete constructor, verifier, native-runtime or workspace
certificate. This draft and its checks stay under ignored `target/`; no frozen
artifact or production source is modified.

## Source and image identity

The successful current-candidate build-v2 receipt and stack capture are retained
under `docs/validation/parliament_ballot_current_candidate/`. Their fixed
manifest, every listed artifact, selected binary hashes and captured disassembly,
function-owner and native-stub identities are checked before and after analysis.
The prior compiled-correspondence bundle is also authenticated. Its algorithms
are not executed again and its old identities are not refreshed.

The source is the exact `framing.rs` retained by the frontend evidence, SHA256
`784c28ba9a115cb2775d78f7147fdcbd1fac5835affd1ef9938a7f1a2c2fc0a0`.
That same identity appears in the successful build source map. It bounds an
opaque field at 256 bytes before `to_vec`. The helper resolves every recorded
build identity. The old Cargo.lock is resolved through the manifest-bound
`build-v2/generated-source/root-Cargo.lock.template.template`; its observed live
replacement is reported separately. Every other build input must still match its
recorded identity. This does not establish compiler correctness or an exhaustive
native dependency closure. Checkout applicability remains false even if a later
checkout happens to match these selected observations.

The bodies occupy 156 bytes / 39 instructions at portable `0x100013b28` and
accelerated `0x100013b2c`. The helper extracts their bytes from the file-backed
Mach-O text section. It trusts the authenticated tool's disassembly-to-image
correspondence; it does not implement a general ARM decoder. Numeric call targets
must resolve to the captured allocator, shim, allocation-error function or the
`_memcpy` import. The instruction graph and argument proof are newly derived
from the instruction operands, not supplied by the body hash or earlier counts.

## Semantic boundary

The entry has a valid immutable input borrow, a valid separate result object,
and available private stack. Input, result, stack and any new allocation are
disjoint. The length word is analyzed for the entire unsigned 64-bit range;
valid-slice provenance remains a premise. The registration caller's admitted
425,984-byte bound is included in that partition.

Ordinary callees obey their Rust/native contracts, preserve saved registers,
stack and unrelated live memory, and do not reenter or asynchronously mutate the
private state. The allocator either returns null or fresh suitably aligned owned
storage for the requested layout. Native `memcpy` reads the supplied input and
writes exactly the requested disjoint destination bytes. Its zero-length call
permits aligned dangling destination 1 without accessing it. None of those
contracts gives a work, allocation, stack/TLS or physical-time bound for a callee.

The interpreter tracks the original input, result and allocated-owner tokens;
the input length and its unsigned interval; saved-register stack stores/restores;
result field writes; and volatile-register clobbering at each call. CMP/unsigned
B.LS and CBZ split intervals or allocator return cases. Every local instruction
must be reached by one of the derived classes, and the graph must be acyclic.
Unrecognized operations, targets, output layouts, control escapes, stack changes
or unproved arguments refuse. This is a deliberately narrow interpreter for
these bodies, not a general machine-code verification framework.

## Derived paths

| Path | Length | Local instructions | Calls in order |
| --- | --- | ---: | --- |
| Oversize rejection | 257 through `2^64-1` | 20 | None |
| Empty success | 0 | 23 | `memcpy(1, input, 0)` |
| Allocated success | 1 through 256 | 29 | shim; `alloc(length, 1)`; `memcpy(allocated, input, length)` |
| Null allocation prefix | 1 through 256 | 18 | shim; `alloc(length, 1)`; `handle_error(1, length)` |

The oversize branch dominates both allocation and copying: the original length
comparison occurs before any such call. The empty path makes no allocator or
shim call, but still makes the native zero-byte copy call. It is not a claim
that native copying allocates nothing internally.

Success writes a result tag of zero and transfers `(capacity, pointer, length)`
as `(length, allocated, length)` or `(0, 1, 0)`. Oversize rejection writes the
error tag, bounded-field error variant, bound 256 and original actual length.
All ordinary paths restore the saved registers, link register and stack. The
constructor performs no deallocation; ownership of a successful vector passes
to the caller. Its later destruction already belongs to caller cleanup terms.
The source remains borrowed; preservation is conditional on the explicit
memory/noninterference contracts, not an independent universal provenance proof.

The last instruction is an allocation-error `BL` at relative offset `0x98`.
Its hypothetical normal return lands at `+0x9c`, the next `frame_bytes` function
entry. There is no RET or BRK there within the constructor. The checker stops
the local prefix at the call and retains its callee work, hypothetical
fallthrough and exceptional continuation as unknown. Neither the symbol name
nor a Rust never-return declaration silently removes that obligation.

## Composition

Let `H`, `A[256]`, and `M[256]` be nonnegative upper costs for the shim, allocator
and native copy, with the latter two covering every permitted argument up to
256 bytes. Their values remain unknown; no monotonicity of native cost is
assumed. On paths on which the constructor and its callees return ordinarily,

```text
O_return <= 29 + H + A[256] + M[256].
```

The null-allocation prefix contributes 18 local instructions plus shim and
allocator work before its final error call. Completing that path additionally
requires error cost `E` and continuation cost `J`; both remain unknown, including
unwind/abort or hypothetical ordinary fallthrough. Thus this does not replace a
universal `O` term by a finite number.

For ordinary caller paths where all five constructor calls return, the earlier
registration-decoder formula can substitute five copies of the expression:
its local 621 becomes 766, while adding `5H + 5A[256] + 5M[256]` in place of
`5O[425984]`. Earlier parser, body-copy, first inlined opaque-field construction,
statement constructor, allocator, native copy and cleanup terms remain. The
five new terms are substitutions, not an extra charge on top of `5O`. Error and
exceptional paths retain their additional frontier obligations.

The local frame is 48 bytes at each call; with the earlier conservative
128-byte Apple red-zone allowance, the local envelope is 176 bytes. For ordinary
paths, with callee additional peaks `S_H`, `S_A`, `S_M`,

```text
S_O_return <= max(176, 48 + max(S_H, S_A, S_M)).
```

At the error frontier, `48 + S_E` and the still-unknown post-return/exceptional
continuation must also be retained. Five sequential calls do not mean five
simultaneous frames. In the decoder's stack formula the constructor's children
have live caller depth `1584+48=1632`; its own `1584+176=1760` is dominated by
the existing 1824-byte decoder/parser envelope. Other direct callees and parser
children keep their existing depths, as do the enclosing frontend and adapter.
The component supplies no complete numerical stack or 64 MiB certificate.

## Validation proposal

The separate staged test module uses an independent four-path calculation,
exact argument/result expectations and mutations of guards, branches, pointer
and length registers, call arguments, result stores, frame restoration and the
final error continuation. Source/archive/image/stub identity changes and output
overwrite must refuse. Tests must retain actual outcomes before publication.

The helper writes only a new report, emits UNKNOWN on a failed analysis, and
refuses qualification with exit 2 even after a successful conditional analysis.
All complete-constructor, complete-native/workspace, physical-cost and
profile/production flags remain false. TODO: discharge lower native and
allocator contracts and costs, error and exceptional continuations, complete
allocation correspondence, independent review and named-hardware qualification.
